 <section xml:id="Section-2">
      <title>Flipping Coins</title>

      <p>Here is a common quant interview style question. Suppose you flip a coin until you get heads. How many flips do you expect this to take?</p>

      <p>Here the random variable is <m>X</m>, where <m>X(s)</m> is the
       number of flips it takes to get heads. The probability space is
        infinite. Here we want essentially,</p>
      <me>\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} i P(\text{First Heads after }i\text{ flips}).</me>
      
      <p> To be clear, the after <m>i</m> flips means that the first <m>i-1</m> 
      flips were tails and the <m>i</m>-th flip was heads.
      You could compute this directly, or we could use linearity 
      of expectation. You can compute this directly as an infinite series.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-first-heads-probability">
        <p>Explain why the probability of the first heads appearing after <m>i</m> flips is <m>1/2^{i}</m>.</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
      </exercise>

      <p>Using the result of the exercise, you get the expectation</p>
      <me>\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} i (1/2)^{i},</me>
      <p>and then you could use calculus tricks to compute it. But maybe you're looking at that and don't remember what a so-called “calculus” is. Instead you think there must be some better way. Indeed there is.</p>

      <theorem xml:id="theorem-first-heads-expectation">
        <statement>
          <p>The expected number of flips until reaching a first heads is <m>2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>You can split <m>X</m> into two random variables. Let <m>Y</m> be a variable that is
           <m>1</m> if you flip heads first and <m>0</m> otherwise. Let <m>Z</m> be a random variable that is <m>0</m> if you flip heads first and the number of flips until you reached your first heads otherwise.</p>

          <p><alert>Claim 1</alert>: <m>X = Y + Z</m>.</p>
          <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

          <p><alert>Claim 2:</alert> <m>\mathbb{E}[Y] = 1/2</m></p>
          <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

          <p><alert>Claim 3:</alert> <m>\mathbb{E}[Z] = 1/2(\mathbb{E}[X]+1)</m>.</p>
          <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>
